fact WorkItem(id: "WI-735", description: some(value: "WI-714/WI-727 follow-up: fix -- accept NON-LITERAL constant arguments (a const-folded expression / a term-level const binding), beyond the literal Int/Float/Bool/String the first increment allows. CONTEXT: proposal 052 'fix is sugar' + proposal 056 (variadic capture), delivered as WI-727 (94dbe449). 'r.fix(x: 1, z: 2)' restricts columns to constants and DROPS them (= where(eq(x,1)) + project). fix is an ORDINARY operation -- 'operation fix[R](p: Relation, ...args: R) -> Relation[T = Without[T = p.T, Drop = R], E = p.E]' -- whose dynamic column arguments ride the ...args: R variadic capture into a named-tuple record; the host builtin (relation_fix) reads the args record VALUE and wraps guarded(query, eq(col, const)) per captured field, then drops the column (which stays SOLVED in the query = bag semantics, like project). SCOPE of the first increment: LITERAL constants only. GAP: a captured arg that is a const-folded expression or a term-level const (proposal 039 / WI-084, which already carries a LOAD-time purity gate) is rejected even though it is statically a constant. FIX DIRECTION: relax the constant check to accept any value the const/purity machinery already certifies as a compile-time constant -- REUSE WI-084's existing gate rather than adding a second notion of constant-ness; the eq is a GUARDED SEMANTIC eq (PartialEq.eq, WI-616), so any value with a lawful eq works at runtime; the constraint is about compile-time knowability, not the eq. Keep the Without[T, Drop] membership + constant-TYPE checks as the LOAD errors, and keep NOTHING keyed on fix's identity in the typer (the feedback-typer-no-nongeneric-code discipline -- the op_sym == Relation.fix recognizer was explicitly rejected by the user and removed). ACCEPTANCE: 'r.fix(age: someConstOp())' / a term-level const binding restricts + drops correctly end-to-end; a non-constant (a runtime value) stays a LOUD load error naming why; existing wi727_fix tests (11) green; cargo-test green via scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-714", "WI-727"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Rejected(reason: "premise refuted by measurement: fix has no literal/constant gate to relax — its argument is already an ordinary expression (see the feedback)", at: "2026-08-17T20:20:01Z"))

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-735", author: "claude", content: "PREMISE REFUTED — MEASURED, no code change needed. There is no literal/constant gate in fix to relax. (1) The capture is unconstrained (proposal 056 OQ table row 4: 'none on the capture'); normalize_variadic_capture types each leftover named arg as an ORDINARY expression. (2) The only load checks are in without_named_tuple_types (typing.rs): the field must name a column, and its type must match the column's. Neither asks what the value IS. (3) relation_fix (eval/builtins.rs) wraps guarded(query, eq(?col, v)) with the evaluated Value — a semantic PartialEq.eq, so any value with a lawful eq restricts. WHERE THE WORD CAME FROM: proposal 052's descriptive prose ('fix — column = constant (sugar)', 'restrict a column to a constant, then drop it') contrasts fix with WHERE — one value for the whole restriction vs a per-ROW predicate. 'Constant' there means ROW-INDEPENDENT, not compile-time-known; and an argument evaluated once at the call is row-independent BY CONSTRUCTION. That prose leaked into relation_fix's doc comment as if it were a constraint on the argument form, and this ticket was written off that comment. DRIVEN, all green on the untouched tree: wi727_fix_value_may_be_a_computed_expression (an operation call), wi727_fix_value_may_be_a_nominal_entity (a user-sort entity via an op), and the CONTROL wi727_fix_value_may_be_a_runtime_parameter — the fixed value is the enclosing operation's PARAMETER, so ONE call site gives names_at(30) = [alice, carol] and names_at(25) = [bob]; a literal-only fix could not pass that. Doc comments in eval/builtins.rs and the wi727_fix_test.rs header corrected to say VALUE, with the row-independent reading stated at the site.", at: "2026-08-17T20:19:51Z")

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-735", author: "claude", content: "CORRECTION TO THE REJECTION ABOVE — THE REJECTION'S VERDICT ON fix STANDS, ITS SCOPE DID NOT. Found by /code-review, then measured. The literal gate this ticket named IS REAL; it lives ONE COORDINATE OVER, in `where`/`join`'s row-lambda operand compiler (`compile_operand`, the `guarded_of` / `conjoin_of` compile-time macros), not in `fix`. This ticket's phrase 'beyond the literal Int/Float/Bool/String the first increment allows' is VERBATIM that compiler's error message — so the author was reading the where gate and mis-filed it against fix, which is documented as '≡ where(...) + project'. I measured fix ONLY and never probed the sibling, so 'premise refuted, no code change needed' was half right and, by closing the ticket, briefly left a real gap with no owner. MEASURED (probe): 'r.where(lambda c -> eq(c.age, 30))' loads (CONTROL); 'let v = 30' + 'eq(c.age, v)' and 'eq(c.age, thirty())' BOTH fail at load with 'guarded_of cannot expand this expression: expected a column (c.x) or a literal ... got an operand that is neither a row column nor a literal'. RE-FILED AS WI-1127, from the real site, with the shared-caller census (compile_condition -> compile_operand is reached by BOTH guarded_of/where_run AND conjoin_of/join_run, so the gate binds join too) and the LINQ fix direction. WI-735 STAYS REJECTED: nothing about fix changes, and re-scoping this description in place would have hidden which construct actually has the gate. ALSO CORRECTED, from the same review: the stdlib doc on the `operation fix` declaration (stdlib/anthill/prelude/relation.anthill) still carried the 'constants' wording — the previous feedback claimed the doc sweep was done after naming only 2 of its 3 producers, and the most-read one, the user-facing declaration, was the one missed; relation_fix's own loop had rebuilt the word 25 lines under its corrected header (const_val -> fixed_val); the 'fix ≡ where + project' equivalence is now written '≈' with the WI-1127 reason at all three sites, since it is FALSE for exactly the non-literal values fix accepts and where refuses; and three test gaps are closed — a let-bound value (a form the doc named but nothing drove), a COMPOUND entity value (the shipped nominal test uses a nullary atom, where eq compares atoms and never recurses), and the Without type gate driven through the non-literal channel. Back-out measured on that last one (`if false &&` on types_compatible): it and the literal type-mismatch test fail, the other 15 in the file pass either way.", at: "2026-08-17T23:02:05Z")
